{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["The reason for <b>\"You are standing this day before YHWH your Elohim\"</b> — you are standing and prepared before HaShem in order to enter into His covenant, for they were assembled to receive the Torah in its full exposition. Or, they were standing before the Ark, and the covenant is the oath and the imprecation which he will mention: \"that you may enter into the covenant of YHWH your Elohim, and into His oath.\" And it is possible that he forged yet another covenant with them like the first covenant which he made with them at Mount Sinai (Exodus 24:5–8), where he offered burnt offerings over them and took half the blood to sprinkle on the altar and half the blood he sprinkled on the people — but there was no need to mention this. And by way of truth, I have already made known to you this covenant." "And the reason for \"your heads, your tribes\" — \"your heads\" means \"the heads of your tribes,\" like \"and the prophecy, [namely] the prophecy of Oded the prophet\" — according to the opinion of R. Abraham ibn Ezra (on Deuteronomy 29:9). In my own opinion, the verse includes and then specifies: the general category is \"your heads and all your tribes,\" and it returns and specifies \"your elders and your officers,\" who are your heads — for an elder and one held in honor is the head. And so it says (Deuteronomy 31:28), \"Assemble to me all the elders of your tribes and your officers,\" for every man who has authority and appointment over the community is called a \"head.\" It is said of the princes (Numbers 1:16) <b>\"the heads of the thousands of Israel\"</b>, for they hold the highest rank among all the thousands of Israel; and it is said of the spies (Numbers 13:3) <b>\"they were heads of the children of Israel\"</b>, that they were heads among the children of Israel, not heads over all the children of Israel — and all of them are included in \"your heads.\" Then it returns and says \"every man of Israel,\" who are \"your tribes,\" and it further mentions the little ones and the women and the strangers and the woodcutters and the water drawers, who constituted a great multitude. And it brought the little ones there in order to bring them into the covenant, for the covenant was also forged with the future generations, or in order to merit them, in the same sense as our Sages said regarding Hakhel (Hagigah 3a). And our Sages said (Yevamot 79a) that some Canaanites came in the days of Moses just as they came in the days of Joshua, and he assigned them as woodcutters and water drawers for the congregation and for the Tabernacle of YHWH. And the intention is not to say that they deceived him, but rather they came to him to make peace with him, for such is the law, as we have explained (above, Deuteronomy 20:11). And thus I found in Midrash Tanchuma (Nitzavim 2): \"to teach you that they came to Moses and he did not accept them\" — that is to say, they could not deceive him into forging a covenant with them, but he immediately made them woodcutters and water drawers."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 29, :notes {0 "The phrase 'I have already made known to you' refers the reader to Ramban's earlier kabbalistic comments on this covenant, left cryptic as is his practice."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T02:56:40.921390592Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}